// The paper, and the order a power-on happens in.
//
// There is no terminal here that owns the screen. The screen is the LCD
// controller's output, and the LCD controller is a device on the bus like any
// other, so the stage's job is only to start the machine and get out of the
// way: one sprite holds the CPU and every device it can reach, and the script
// that runs it never returns until the guest stops.
//
// The order matters, and it is a real power-on order:
//
//   1. The machine resets every device and lays the boot ROM and the guest
//      images where the memory map says they live.
//   2. The monitor (in the other build, `src/monitor/`) starts drawing the
//      LCD's scanout.
//   3. Only then does the CPU come out of reset.
//
// The backdrop is the panel's own black: the Stage is the glass, so nothing
// draws on it that the controller did not scan out.

stage {
    costume "paper" = "assets/backdrop.svg";

    broadcast "Machine.powerOn";
    broadcast "Machine.run";
    broadcast "Monitor.run";

    on flag_clicked {
        events::broadcast_and_wait("Machine.powerOn");
        events::broadcast("Monitor.run");
        events::broadcast("Machine.run");
    }
}
